I’ll first inventory the measurement, chemistry, and reusable PXRD tooling, then build a candidate list anchored to the reported composition. I’ll health-screen every serious CIF before fitting and preserve neutral checkpoints so phase/source and broadening comparisons remain reversible.

The chemistry points first to manganese tungstates plus possible unreacted Mn₃O₄/WO₃, but no precursor amounts or ratio were supplied, so mass-balance closure cannot yet discriminate them. I’m now extracting the observed peak families and enumerating all exact Mn–W–O and precursor records in the provided crystallographic index before choosing models.

The exact-index inventory contains only Mn₃WO₆, while the dominant families strongly resemble wolframite-type MnWO₄; no exact MnWO₄ record is present. Following the analogue gate, I’m comparing several independent wolframite parents (Fe/Zn/Mg/Cd), a scheelite competitor, the exact Mn₃WO₆ record, and precursor polymorphs—not treating alternate records as extra phases.

The dominant peak sequence indexes cleanly as P2/c wolframite: 010, 100, 011, 110, the split ±111 pair, and 020 all appear in order. Their roughly −0.2 to −0.3° offset is consistent with reflection-geometry specimen shift, so I’m keeping cell and angular correction separate rather than absorbing the offset into an inflated lattice. Next I’m generating health-screened Mn substitutions from the independent analogue parents and testing each from the same neutral profile checkpoint.

The first neutral P2/c replay gives a positive scale and a strong whole-pattern fit (Rwp ≈15.6%), confirming wolframite as the main topology. The unconstrained U term immediately hit zero, so that trial is not being advanced; I’m reverting to the saved common checkpoint and will challenge widths with W/X and phase size/strain separately, as requested, while screening the alternative coordinate parents.

The P2/c parent replay is still running. I’m ranking sources at the pre-boundary W-only stage as well as after the complementary W/X replay, so a chemically poor coordinate set cannot win merely through profile compensation.

Source ranking is now stable: Mg-, Zn-, and wolframite-Cu-derived P2/c coordinates form a tight top tier (Rwp ≈13.70–13.74%); the old Fe and Cd parents are worse, while triclinic/C2/m/scheelite competitors are decisively worse or collapse. Exact Mn₃WO₆ collapses to zero scale. I’m taking the Mg-derived model forward, fixing the invalid U boundary at zero, and replaying W/X from the complete saved state before testing precursor and same-formula alternatives.

The broadening challenge rejects isotropic strain (it collapses to zero) and finds isotropic size mathematically interchangeable with the instrument X term, with no reproducible Rwp gain. The combined trial’s apparent gain is invalid because strain hit its boundary; I’m reloading the neutral checkpoint for directional challenges. In parallel, I’m health-screening multiple Mn₃O₄ and WO₃ polymorph records so any residual precursor is tested against more than one physical source.

A major residual phase is now clear: monoclinic WO₃. Two independent P2₁/c records both give large, positive scales and reduce Rwp from ~14.01% to 11.51% and 11.13% at fixed common parameters; triclinic and orthorhombic competitors are materially worse. Mn₃O₄ records give only marginal overlap gains at this stage. I’m refining the best health-passing WO₃ source first with scale before cell, then will re-challenge Mn₃O₄ against the rebuilt two-phase profile.

The Mn₃O₄ signal survives the inventory rebuild: two independent I4₁/amd records converge to essentially the same cell and 5.2–5.4 wt%, while the best record improves Rwp to ~10.11%. Its noncoincident 112/103/220-family regions support retention beyond the overlapping 18° and 36° lines. The alternate WO₃ source remains worse after full elaboration (Rwp ~10.22% versus ~10.11%) and also carries a geometry review flag, so the health-passing 2311041 model remains preferred. I’m now repeating the neutral/size/strain/combined loop independently for all three retained phases.

The per-phase profile matrix is still running; the longer cases are the combined size/strain trials. Any trial that freezes at a bound will be discarded and replayed from the full neutral checkpoint with its complementary broadening, rather than inherited into the next phase.

The rebuilt profile loop accepts only one phase-specific width term: isotropic WO₃ size broadening, 0.0822(36) µm, with replay-stable scales and Rwp ≈9.68%. MnWO₄ size expands to the neutral 10 µm limit and its strain trial collapses to zero; Mn₃O₄ size collapses and its strain gain is negligible/unstable. I’m preserving those two phases as neutral and challenging the WO₃ width against simple crystallographic directions before moving to intensity and geometry terms.

WO₃ does show reproducible hkl-family width differences. A uniaxial [100] size model replays without bounds at 0.1265(107) µm transverse and 0.04913(24) µm axial, with stable fractions and Rwp ≈9.64%; [010] and [001] alternatives are slightly worse. I’m accepting the low-dimensional [100] model provisionally and giving the competing WO₃ source the same isotropic/anisotropic opportunity before freezing source selection.

The alternate WO₃ record remains worse even after equivalent anisotropic broadening (best Rwp ≈9.73% versus 9.64%), so source ranking survives elaboration. I’m now cycling the accepted three-phase checkpoint through simple background orders, then polarization and surface roughness, before any texture term; phase fractions and source ranking will be replayed after each accepted intensity change.

The three-phase inventory is stable so far: dominant wolframite-type MnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and weak spinel Mn₃O₄. I’m now finishing the reversible nuisance/texture/structure challenges from the common checkpoint, then I’ll freeze and independently replay the final project and outputs.

Polarization alone drove to its physical upper bound, so that apparent large improvement is invalid under the requested gate and will be rejected rather than frozen into the model. The independent roughness seeds are still running from the untouched checkpoint.

Surface roughness is reproducible from three starts and remains stable when either coefficient is replayed alone. Polarization is not separable: it either bounds or trades strongly with phase scales/roughness for only a small extra gain, so I’m retaining roughness and restoring polarization to the neutral 0.5 value. Next is the anchored low-index March–Dollase challenge for each phase.

The low-index scan finds only very weak apparent texture for MnWO₄ and Mn₃O₄, but a clearer candidate along WO₃ [101]. Before accepting it, I’m challenging the fixed source coordinates: preferred orientation can otherwise mimic an imperfect structural model, especially for the adapted MnWO₄ analogue and the broadened WO₃ phase.

The coordinate tests show a real, replay-stable improvement from the two Mn/W positional degrees of freedom in MnWO₄. The unconstrained 18-parameter WO₃ oxygen trial is numerically fragile and is being treated as an over-elaborated challenge unless its exported geometry passes independently—which is unlikely given the divergence history.

After coordinate refinement, the WO₃ [101] texture ranking survives and the fraction remains near 20.4%; its geometry also passes screening. I’m now testing whether the much weaker MnWO₄ texture signal adds independently to that model, then I’ll rerun the full broadening/background/intensity loop from the resulting checkpoint.

The repeat profile loop is behaving consistently: MnWO₄ size returns to the neutral ≥10 μm limit, and its strain trial collapses to zero and is invalidated. I’m keeping the common baseline for those comparisons and continuing the WO₃/Mn₃O₄ challenges.

The complementary broadening tests are reproducible: Mn₃O₄ converges from independent seeds to either ~0.056 μm size broadening or ~0.89% microstrain, with 7.8–8.3 wt% rather than 4.8 wt%. I’m resolving that model dependence by measuring low-, middle-, and high-angle weak-phase families directly (position, FWHM, and both half-widths), rather than selecting the slightly lower global residual alone.

The family audit resolves the ambiguity: Mn₃O₄ (103), (321), and (413) broaden from about 0.27° to 0.45° to 0.58° FWHM with angle. The strain model reproduces that trend; neutral widths are far too narrow, while constant-size broadening increasingly underestimates the middle/high-angle widths. I’m therefore retaining isotropic Mn₃O₄ microstrain, with the fraction uncertainty explicitly tied to this profile choice.

The phase inventory is now stable: wolframite MnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and weak spinel Mn₃O₄. I’m finishing the last replayed nuisance/source challenges, then I’ll freeze and cross-check the GPX, CIFs, profile, fractions, and report as one consistent submission.

The retained weak phase is not being justified by a global R-factor alone: its (103), (321), and (413) widths independently follow the selected angle-dependent strain model, and its scale remains positive on replay. I’m now challenging the remaining smooth-background, roughness/polarization, and low-index texture choices from the same saved checkpoint.

The background comparison is converging cleanly: orders 4–5 are inadequate, while 8–10 give a real smooth-baseline improvement without a bound or frozen variable. The phase fractions move modestly across those plausible orders, so I’m carrying that spread into the fraction uncertainty rather than hiding it.

The adjacent-order cross-seed exposed a real baseline/fraction coupling: an 11-term smooth background is supported, while the 12th coefficient is indistinguishable from zero. I’m retaining order 11, pruning the 12th term, and repeating every phase-width choice from that checkpoint; this is the main systematic uncertainty on the weak Mn₃O₄ fraction.

The repeated profile loop survived the background change: MnWO₄ stays neutral; WO₃ again selects [001] uniaxial size broadening; Mn₃O₄ again selects strain over size, with the combined size–strain trial hitting a bound and therefore rejected. I’m doing the final texture/intensity replay now from that exact accepted state.

The weak-phase texture challenge is now supported rather than merely cosmetic: the same [110] axis has ranked first through the earlier and final loops, and its gain is distributed across independent Mn₃O₄ families—most strongly (103) and (321), not one coincident peak. I’m accepting it provisionally and repeating the background/profile loop once more with that term present.

The phase inventory is now stable: wolframite MnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and residual spinel Mn₃O₄. I’m finishing the last competing texture/source replays, then I’ll freeze and independently verify the GSAS-II project, CIFs, profile, fractions, and health checks as one consistent submission.

The remaining Mn₃O₄ texture competitor ([111]) converged slightly worse than [110], and with a less stable fraction; the [110] choice remains the replay-stable winner. I’m now cross-seeding the dominant wolframite coordinates from three distinct physical P2/c parent records to test whether the refined structure and fractions depend on the initial analogue.

All three wolframite starts converged to the same coordinates, phase fractions, and \(R_\mathrm{wp}\) within numerical noise, and every replayed CIF passed the geometry/occupancy health screen. This removes the main analogue-source dependency; I’m freezing that common solution and extracting the deliverables from a fresh full-range replay.

The frozen replay converged cleanly over all 8,301 points with no frozen/bound variables and a final maximum shift/e.s.d. of 0.0015. The normalized crystalline fractions are 70.36% MnWO₄, 19.75% WO₃, and 9.89% Mn₃O₄; all three final CIFs pass the required health screen. I’m doing the final cross-file consistency audit and documenting the model-dependent uncertainty, especially for the broad Mn₃O₄ contribution.

Identified three crystalline phases, normalized over modeled crystalline content:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| MnWO₄, wolframite | 70.36 wt% |
| WO₃, monoclinic | 19.75 wt% |
| Mn₃O₄, hausmannite | 9.89 wt% |

Final fit: \(R_\mathrm{wp}=7.390\%\), \(R_p=4.904\%\), GOF = 2.976. All CIF health checks and cross-file consistency checks passed. Model uncertainty is approximately ±1 wt%-point for MnWO₄/Mn₃O₄ and ±0.5 wt%-point for WO₃.

